<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:vm="using:Parakeet.App.ViewModels"
        xmlns:views="using:Parakeet.App.Views"
        xmlns:sys="using:System"
        x:Class="Parakeet.App.Views.MainWindow"
        x:DataType="vm:MainWindowViewModel"
        Title="Uindosill — local transcription"
        Width="1080" Height="720"
        MinWidth="820" MinHeight="520"
        Background="{DynamicResource GroundBrush}"
        Foreground="{DynamicResource InkBrush}"
        FontFamily="{DynamicResource SansFamily}"
        ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint="True"
        WindowDecorations="BorderOnly">

  <!--
    The interface designed 2026-08-19, in the shape that design specifies: no OS title bar, a 46px
    headerbar carrying the application name and a pill view-switcher, and square corners on
    everything that is not a circle.

    Colours, type and control geometry are not here — they are in Theme/Tokens.axaml and
    Theme/Controls.axaml, which reach the whole application. What is here is layout and the few
    places a surface has to name its own colour because it is the only one of its kind.

    Two structural notes, because both look like accidents:

    · The pages still live in a TabControl, whose header strip is collapsed by the `headless`
      class. The design puts the switcher in the middle of the headerbar, between the application
      name and the window buttons, which is not a place a tab strip can be — so the switcher is
      four radio buttons bound to the same SelectedTab index the TabControl reads. The TabControl
      is doing the page-switching; the pills are the only part anyone sees.

    · The corner is square by asking rather than by drawing. See Services/WindowCorner.cs — DWM
      takes a preference, not a radius, which is what makes square reachable where the earlier
      12px version of this design was not. Confirmed on Windows 11 at 250% scaling: DWM reports
      the do-not-round preference back, and the window draws square.

    · WindowDecorations="BorderOnly" removes the toolkit's own title bar, and the headerbar below
      takes its place through WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole.

      BorderOnly and NOT None, which is the trap here. None removes the whole frame along with the
      title bar: it takes WS_THICKFRAME with it, so the window stops being resizable, and it takes
      the compositor's shadow, so on a light desktop nothing shows where the application ends.
      Both were shipped that way for an afternoon and neither is visible in a screenshot of the
      window — the style bits have to be read off the handle. Without that the caption and a
      second set of window buttons are drawn over this design's own — invisible to a headless
      render, because there is no window frame there to draw, and found only by photographing the
      built application. The roles also mean the platform handles dragging and
      double-click-to-maximise, rather than a PointerPressed handler calling BeginMoveDrag.

      The cost of that arrangement: inside the TitleBar element every press is a drag unless the
      control under the pointer carries a role of its own. The window buttons have native ones
      (MinimizeButton, MaximizeButton, CloseButton); anything else interactive placed in the
      headerbar — the tab switcher — has to be marked ElementRole="User", Avalonia's carve-out
      for "this receives input even inside chrome". Without it the control renders, hovers and
      never clicks, and a headless test cannot tell: there is no platform hit-test in it.
  -->

  <Window.Resources>
    <views:IndexMatchConverter x:Key="IndexMatch" />
    <!-- The search hit's ground comes from the token sheet rather than from a hex in the
         converter, so taro has one definition. StaticResource because a converter is a plain
         object with no styled properties for DynamicResource to track — the brush is resolved
         once, at load, out of the application's dictionary. -->
    <views:HighlightConverter x:Key="Highlight" Hit="{StaticResource Taro200Brush}" />
  </Window.Resources>

  <!-- The window's edge. Inside the window because there is nothing outside it to draw on: with
       WindowDecorations="None" the frame is gone, so this hairline and the compositor's shadow are
       the only things saying where the application stops. -->
  <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource WindowEdgeBrush}"
          BorderThickness="1"
          Background="{DynamicResource GroundBrush}">
  <DockPanel>

    <!-- ══ Headerbar ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         46px, one hairline underneath, no fill. Dragging it moves the window, which is the whole
         job the OS title bar was doing before it was turned off. -->
    <Border DockPanel.Dock="Top"
            Name="HeaderBar"
            WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="TitleBar"
            Height="46"
            Background="{DynamicResource GroundBrush}"
            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource RuleSeenBrush}"
            BorderThickness="0,0,0,1"
            Padding="14,0,0,0">
      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="210,*,210">

        <!-- The mark and the name. matcha-100 ground, matcha-700 strokes: the application's own
             badge is the one place the accent appears without meaning "selected". -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="0" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="9" VerticalAlignment="Center">
          <Border Width="22" Height="22" Background="{DynamicResource Matcha100Brush}">
            <Path Stroke="{DynamicResource Matcha700Brush}" StrokeThickness="2.4"
                  StrokeLineCap="Round"
                  Data="M4 11.5v1M8.5 7v10M13 3.5v17M17.5 8.5v7M22 11.5v1"
                  Width="13" Height="13" Stretch="Uniform"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </Border>
          <TextBlock Text="Uindosill" FontSize="13" FontWeight="Bold"
                     LetterSpacing="-0.156" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          <TextBlock Text="local transcription" Classes="meta" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- The switcher. Licences last, because it is the one tab nobody opens twice.

             ElementRole="User" because this sits inside the TitleBar element, and inside that
             element the platform answers every press as "start dragging the window" unless the
             control under the pointer says otherwise. The pills carry no native role of their own
             — the window buttons do, which is why they clicked and these did not — so they are
             marked as the interactive carve-out Avalonia provides for exactly this: input reaches
             them instead of being taken for a move. A headless render cannot show the difference;
             only a pointer on the built window can. -->
        <Border Grid.Column="1" Classes="switcher"
                WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="User"
                HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center">
          <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="2">
            <RadioButton Name="TabTranscribe" Classes="segment" GroupName="Switcher" Content="Transcribe"
                         IsChecked="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=0}" />
            <!-- Ask sits next to Transcribe because it acts on what Transcribe produces, and last
                 in the TabControl because the four before it are indexed where tests and settings
                 already found them. The switcher's order and the TabControl's have never been the
                 same thing — Licences is index 2 and shown last — so this is the existing
                 arrangement rather than a new exception to it. -->
            <RadioButton Name="TabAsk" Classes="segment" GroupName="Switcher" Content="Ask"
                         IsChecked="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=4}" />
            <RadioButton Name="TabModels" Classes="segment" GroupName="Switcher" Content="Models"
                         IsChecked="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=1}" />
            <RadioButton Name="TabUpdates" Classes="segment" GroupName="Switcher" Content="Updates"
                         IsChecked="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=3}" />
            <RadioButton Name="TabLicences" Classes="segment" GroupName="Switcher" Content="Licences"
                         IsChecked="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=2}" />
          </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <!-- Bare glyphs, no ground until hover. Spacing is 0 because each button is 40px wide
             around an 11px glyph, so the gap a reader sees is the padding rather than a margin. -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="0"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Stretch">
          <!-- Stretch="None" on all three, and the minimise glyph is why: its geometry is a
               horizontal line, so it is seven units wide and zero tall, and scaling that uniformly
               into a 9x9 box has no defined answer — Avalonia drew a dot. Left at their natural
               size the three glyphs are also consistent with each other, which uniform scaling of
               three different aspect ratios was not. -->
          <Button Name="WindowMinimise" WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="MinimizeButton" Classes="window" ToolTip.Tip="Minimise">
            <Path StrokeThickness="1.3"
                  StrokeLineCap="Round" Data="M0 0h11" Stretch="None" />
          </Button>
          <!-- Two overlapping squares rather than one: the restore glyph, drawn as a filled-edge
               front square with the top-right of a second showing behind it. Deliberately the same
               glyph in both window states — a button that changes shape under the pointer is a
               button people misclick, and the tooltip says which way it goes. -->
          <Button Name="WindowMaximise" WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="MaximizeButton" Classes="window" ToolTip.Tip="Maximise">
            <Path StrokeThickness="1.3"
                  StrokeJoin="Round" StrokeLineCap="Round"
                  Data="M0 3.2h7.8v7.8h-7.8z M2.9 3.2v-2.9h7.8v7.8h-2.9"
                  Stretch="None" />
          </Button>
          <Button Name="WindowClose" WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="CloseButton" Classes="window close" ToolTip.Tip="Close">
            <Path StrokeThickness="1.3"
                  StrokeLineCap="Round" Data="M0 0l10.5 10.5M10.5 0l-10.5 10.5" Stretch="None" />
          </Button>
        </StackPanel>
      </Grid>
    </Border>

    <!-- ══ Update notice ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         The visible half of the update decision: above the pages rather than inside one, because a
         notice on a tab nobody opens is not a notice, and hidden entirely when there is nothing to
         say, so the normal window is exactly as it was.

         Matcha rather than the blue it used to be. Blue belongs to no generation in this system,
         and an update is a v1 thing. -->
    <Border DockPanel.Dock="Top"
            Name="UpdateNotice"
            IsVisible="{Binding Updates.IsUpdateAvailable}"
            Background="{DynamicResource Matcha50Brush}"
            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Matcha200Brush}"
            BorderThickness="0,0,0,1"
            Padding="16,10">
      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding Updates.Notice}"
                   Foreground="{DynamicResource Matcha700Brush}"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
        <Button Grid.Column="1" Classes="primary" Content="Download and restart"
                Command="{Binding Updates.InstallCommand}"
                IsEnabled="{Binding Updates.CanInstall}" Margin="12,0,0,0" />
      </Grid>
    </Border>

    <TabControl Name="Tabs" Classes="headless" SelectedIndex="{Binding SelectedTab}">

      <!-- ══ Transcribe ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
      <TabItem Header="Transcribe">
        <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" Margin="16" DataContext="{Binding Transcribe}"
              x:DataType="vm:TranscribeViewModel">

          <!-- Shut while a batch runs, because the queue behind it is. A zone that keeps taking
               drops the view model then discards is a gesture accepted and thrown away, and the
               row it used to leave behind sat blank at "Waiting" for ever. Open it is matcha-50
               inside a dashed matcha-400 edge; shut it is the sunken grey with no dash. -->
          <Border Grid.Row="0"
                  Name="DropZone"
                  DragDrop.AllowDrop="{Binding !IsRunning}"
                  Background="{DynamicResource Matcha50Brush}"
                  Margin="0,0,0,12">
            <!-- The dash is a Rectangle rather than the Border's own edge: in Avalonia 12.1.1
                 StrokeDashArray is a Shape property and Border has no dashed variant, so a border
                 here could only ever be solid. Drawn under the content in the same cell, so it
                 does not take part in the layout. -->
            <Panel>
              <Rectangle Stroke="{DynamicResource Matcha400Brush}" StrokeThickness="1"
                         StrokeDashArray="4,3" />
              <StackPanel Spacing="6" HorizontalAlignment="Center" Margin="18">
                <Path Stroke="{DynamicResource Matcha700Brush}" StrokeThickness="1.9"
                      StrokeLineCap="Round" StrokeJoin="Round"
                      Data="M12 3v11m0 0l-4-4m4 4l4-4M4 17v2a2 2 0 002 2h12a2 2 0 002-2v-2"
                      Width="19" Height="19" Stretch="Uniform" HorizontalAlignment="Center" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding DropHint}" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                           FontSize="14.5" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                           Foreground="{DynamicResource Matcha700Brush}" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding SupportedExtensionsHint}" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                           Classes="mono meta" FontSize="10.5"
                           IsVisible="{Binding !IsRunning}" />
              </StackPanel>
            </Panel>
          </Border>

          <Grid Grid.Row="1" ColumnDefinitions="344,14,*">

            <!-- ── The queue ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
            <DockPanel Grid.Column="0">
              <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="7" Margin="0,10,0,0">
                <Button Classes="primary" Content="Start" Command="{Binding StartCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding CanStart}" />
                <!-- Start runs what has not been run, so redoing a finished queue is asked for
                     rather than guessed at: this is the only way to transcribe the same files a
                     second time, and it is live only when there is a finished row to redo. -->
                <Button Content="Run again" Command="{Binding RunAgainCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding CanRunAgain}" />
                <Button Content="Cancel" Command="{Binding CancelCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding IsRunning}" />
                <Button Content="Clear" Command="{Binding ClearCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding !IsRunning}" />
              </StackPanel>

              <!-- Start is disabled until a model is loaded, and again once every file in the
                   queue is transcribed, so it has to say which. -->
              <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Text="{Binding StartHint}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Classes="fine warning" Margin="0,8,0,0"
                         IsVisible="{Binding StartHint, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

              <ListBox ItemsSource="{Binding Jobs}" SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedJob}">
                <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                  <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:JobViewModel">
                    <StackPanel Spacing="5">
                      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding FileName}" Classes="emphasis"
                                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding ProgressLabel}" Classes="meta"
                                   Margin="7,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   IsVisible="{Binding ProgressLabel, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                      </Grid>
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Status}" Classes="meta" />
                      <!-- Gone once the row is finished. A full bar under "Done — 6 files" is a
                           progress report for something that is not in progress, and it made every
                           completed row look like a fifth thing still running. Queued rows keep an
                           empty one, because they are waiting rather than done. -->
                      <ProgressBar Value="{Binding Progress}" Maximum="100"
                                   IsIndeterminate="{Binding IsIndeterminate}"
                                   IsVisible="{Binding !IsFinished}" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Error}" Classes="fine error"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding Error, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Warning}" Classes="fine warning"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding Warning, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                    </StackPanel>
                  </DataTemplate>
                </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
              </ListBox>
            </DockPanel>

            <GridSplitter Grid.Column="1" Background="Transparent" />

            <!-- ── Options and transcript ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
            <DockPanel Grid.Column="2">
              <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Top" Spacing="9" Margin="0,0,0,11">
                <TextBlock Text="OUTPUT FORMATS" Classes="section" />
                <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Formats}">
                  <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                    <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                      <WrapPanel />
                    </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                  <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:OutputFormatViewModel">
                      <CheckBox Content="{Binding DisplayName}" IsChecked="{Binding IsSelected}"
                                Margin="0,0,14,0" />
                    </DataTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                </ItemsControl>

                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                  <TextBlock Text="Output folder" Classes="secondary" Width="88"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                  <TextBox Text="{Binding OutputDirectory}" Width="280"
                           PlaceholderText="beside each input file" />
                  <Button Content="Browse…" Name="BrowseOutput" />
                </StackPanel>

                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="14">
                  <CheckBox Content="Fixed windows instead of speech detection"
                            IsChecked="{Binding UseFixedWindows}" />
                  <TextBlock Text="Segment cap" Classes="secondary" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                  <NumericUpDown Value="{Binding MaxSegmentSeconds}" Minimum="5" Maximum="60"
                                 Increment="5" Width="110" FormatString="0 's'" />
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- There is no spoken-language picker here, and that is a decision rather than an
                     omission. parakeet.cpp's ABI does take a target language, but the tdt
                     checkpoints this catalogue ships are not prompt-conditioned and never read it:
                     four runs over one clip — no hint, `en`, `de`, and the nonexistent `zz` —
                     produced byte-identical output at exit 0, where a prompt-conditioned model
                     would have failed the `zz` decode (docs/UNPROVEN.md § The language hint). A
                     control that changes nothing is the failure this window refuses to ship, and
                     one drawn permanently disabled is still a control nobody can use. It comes
                     back when a checkpoint that reads the hint does.

                     It would not have been a translation control either way: the translator below
                     is many-to-one, told the target and never the source. -->

                <!-- The speaker opt-in. Off by default; disabled with a reason, not hidden, while
                     no labeller is in the build — a control that silently does nothing is the
                     failure this application refuses to ship.

                     Matcha, because labelling speakers is a v1 feature. It sits on its own tinted
                     strip so the opt-in reads as a decision rather than a fourth checkbox. -->
                <Border Classes="tinted" Padding="10,8">
                  <!-- The outer panel stacks vertically, which is what makes the wrapping below
                       work: a StackPanel measures its children with infinite size along the axis it
                       stacks on, so only the horizontal ones break TextWrapping. -->
                  <StackPanel Spacing="7">
                    <!-- DockPanel, not a horizontal StackPanel: a StackPanel measures its children
                         with infinite width on the stacking axis, so TextWrapping on the hint never
                         engaged and the reason the opt-in was disabled ran off the right edge —
                         "…it is a 453 MiB dow". The hint is the whole point of disabling rather than
                         hiding the control, so it has to be readable. -->
                    <DockPanel LastChildFill="True">
                      <CheckBox DockPanel.Dock="Left"
                                Content="Label speakers"
                                FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                Margin="0,0,9,0"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                IsChecked="{Binding LabelSpeakers}"
                                IsEnabled="{Binding CanLabelSpeakers}" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding SpeakerHint}" Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding SpeakerHint, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                    </DockPanel>

                    <!-- The speaker count, and the only repair this product has for a recording
                         longer than the diariser's labels are established on. Hidden rather than
                         disabled while the opt-in is off, which is the one place this window departs
                         from its own convention and does so deliberately: a disabled control is
                         shown when the user cannot reach the thing it needs, and here they can —
                         the checkbox is right above it and ticking it brings this to life. It is a
                         detail of the opt-in, not a setting of its own.

                         Blank does not run — the placeholder says required and Start refuses until
                         a number is here — but there is still no default of two, because the count
                         has to come from the user for the fold to mean anything. The fold this
                         drives is wrong to apply unasked: on one of the eighteen AMI development
                         meetings the two genuinely different speakers who collide least never
                         overlap at all, so a guessed default merges two real people there. -->
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8"
                                IsVisible="{Binding CanSetSpeakerCount}">
                      <TextBlock Text="How many speakers" Classes="secondary" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                      <!-- Wide enough for the placeholder, which is the longest thing this field
                           ever shows. At 128 the spin buttons ate it and it read "estimat", which a
                           headless render would not have caught and the opened window did. -->
                      <NumericUpDown Value="{Binding SpeakerCount}" Minimum="1" Maximum="99"
                                     Increment="1" Width="170" FormatString="0"
                                     PlaceholderText="required" />
                    </StackPanel>

                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding SpeakerCountHint}" Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               IsVisible="{Binding SpeakerCountHint, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

                    <!-- Past where the evidence stops, said before the batch rather than beside the
                         finished transcript. The command line has fired this since the bound was
                         measured; the window reported only the cap, and a cap is an afterwards
                         sentence about what happened rather than a warning about what is not known
                         to work. -->
                    <!-- No MaxWidth: it wraps to the strip like the hint above it, and a paragraph
                         that broke at a different measure from the one directly over it reads as a
                         different column rather than as the next thing to know. -->
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding SpeakerDurationWarning}" Classes="warning"
                               TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               IsVisible="{Binding SpeakerDurationWarning, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </Border>

                <!-- The English opt-in, the second of v1's two, and drawn as the twin of the first:
                     its own tinted strip, off by default, disabled with a reason rather than hidden
                     while its model is not installed.

                     Matcha like the one above, because translating is a v1 feature too. There is
                     no language picker beside it and that is a finding rather than an omission —
                     the checkpoint is many-to-one, told the target and never the source, so there
                     is nothing for a picker to say. The direction is fixed: into English, never
                     out of it. -->
                <Border Classes="tinted" Padding="10,8">
                  <DockPanel LastChildFill="True">
                    <CheckBox DockPanel.Dock="Left"
                              Name="TranslateToEnglish"
                              Content="English version"
                              FontWeight="SemiBold"
                              Margin="0,0,9,0"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center"
                              IsChecked="{Binding TranslateToEnglish}"
                              IsEnabled="{Binding CanTranslate}" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding TranslationHint}" Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center"
                               IsVisible="{Binding TranslationHint, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                  </DockPanel>
                </Border>
              </StackPanel>

              <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Text="{Binding StatusMessage}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Classes="meta" FontSize="11.5" Margin="0,10,0,0" />

              <!-- The transcript/English switcher, and it exists only when there is something to
                   switch to: a run that did not translate gets no switcher rather than a second
                   pill that leads to an empty pane.

                   The same pills as the headerbar's view switcher, which is one of the four shapes
                   in this design that keeps a corner — a pill reads as tappable. Two panes rather
                   than one pane with a toggle, because the point is to compare: the English is
                   produced *beside* the transcript, not instead of it, and the times, the speakers
                   and their chips are the same on both sides.

                   Left-aligned rather than centred: the headerbar's switcher is the window's, and
                   a second centred pill row underneath it would read as a peer of that rather than
                   as a control belonging to the panel below. -->
              <Border DockPanel.Dock="Top" Classes="switcher" Name="TranscriptPaneSwitcher"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Left" Margin="0,0,0,9"
                      IsVisible="{Binding CanShowTranslation}">
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                  <RadioButton Name="PaneTranscript" Classes="segment" GroupName="TranscriptPane"
                               Content="Transcript"
                               IsChecked="{Binding TranscriptPane, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=0}" />
                  <RadioButton Name="PaneEnglish" Classes="segment" GroupName="TranscriptPane"
                               Content="English"
                               IsChecked="{Binding TranscriptPane, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=1}" />
                </StackPanel>
              </Border>

              <!-- The transcript of the selected file: selectable, read-only, one row per segment
                   with the speaker as a chip rather than a "S1: " prefix. The chip is what makes a
                   labelled transcript readable at a glance, and it is a v1 feature, so it is
                   matcha — see the four chip styles in Theme/Controls.axaml. Unlabelled
                   transcripts simply have no chip and read as paragraphs.

                   Bound to VisibleLines rather than to SelectedJob.Lines, because which of the two
                   collections that is depends on the switcher above. -->
              <Border Classes="panel" Padding="15,13">
                <ScrollViewer>
                  <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding VisibleLines}">
                    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                      <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:TranscriptLineViewModel">
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" Margin="0,0,0,9">
                          <Border Grid.Column="0" Margin="0,0,9,0"
                                  IsVisible="{Binding HasSpeaker}"
                                  Classes="speaker"
                                  Classes.chip0="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=0}"
                                  Classes.chip1="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=1}"
                                  Classes.chip2="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=2}"
                                  Classes.chip3="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=3}">
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Speaker}" />
                          </Border>
                          <SelectableTextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Text}"
                                               TextWrapping="Wrap" LineHeight="20.3" />
                        </Grid>
                      </DataTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl>
                </ScrollViewer>
              </Border>
            </DockPanel>
          </Grid>

          <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Margin="0,12,0,0" Classes="fine meta"
                     Text="Every recording is cut into segments of at most 30 seconds before decoding. That is a correctness requirement: Parakeet degrades on long single-pass audio and glues text across chunk boundaries long before it collapses." />
        </Grid>
      </TabItem>

      <!-- ══ Models ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
      <TabItem Header="Models">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="344,14,*" Margin="16" DataContext="{Binding Models}"
              x:DataType="vm:ModelsViewModel">

          <DockPanel Grid.Column="0">
            <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Text="{Binding ModelDirectory}" Classes="mono meta"
                       FontSize="10.5" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,9" />
            <ListBox ItemsSource="{Binding Models}" SelectedItem="{Binding Selected}">
              <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:ModelViewModel">
                  <StackPanel Spacing="5">
                    <!-- Wrapping, not a horizontal row: the diariser's name plus its badge is
                         wider than the 344px list, and in a StackPanel the badge simply ran off
                         the edge — "SPEAKERS" arrived as "SPE". -->
                    <WrapPanel ItemSpacing="6" LineSpacing="4">
                      <!-- MaxWidth so the longest name in the catalogue wraps rather than running
                           off the edge: a WrapPanel hands a child its desired width, and a single
                           line of text desires all of it. The translation entry is the one that
                           needs it. -->
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding DisplayName}" Classes="emphasis"
                                 TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="316"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                      <!-- Which model is in memory, marked in the list itself. "Installed" is true
                           of every downloaded entry and answers a different question. -->
                      <Border Classes="badge solid" IsVisible="{Binding IsLoaded}">
                        <TextBlock Text="LOADED" />
                      </Border>
                      <!-- A model that names speakers rather than writing words: downloadable
                           here, never offered to Load as the engine.

                           Outlined matcha, not the solid purple it was. Speaker labelling is a v1
                           feature and #6B4E9C sits in taro's neighbourhood, so it would have read
                           as a v2 feature the moment an Ask tab exists. The outline is what still
                           separates it from the solid LOADED badge beside it. -->
                      <Border Classes="badge outlined" IsVisible="{Binding !IsTranscriptionModel}">
                        <TextBlock Text="{Binding TaskLabel}" />
                      </Border>
                    </WrapPanel>
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Status}" Classes="meta" />
                    <ProgressBar Value="{Binding Progress}" Maximum="100"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding IsBusy}" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </DataTemplate>
              </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
            </ListBox>
          </DockPanel>

          <GridSplitter Grid.Column="1" Background="Transparent" />

          <ScrollViewer Grid.Column="2">
            <StackPanel Spacing="12" IsVisible="{Binding Selected, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}">

              <StackPanel Spacing="4">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.DisplayName}" Classes="title" />
                <!-- The upstream artefact's own name, in the monospace this design reserves for
                     text you copy rather than read. It is here so that renaming the entry to
                     something a user understands does not cost anyone the ability to check which
                     exact weights are installed against a model card or a run report. -->
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.TechnicalName}" Classes="mono meta"
                           FontSize="10.5" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,1,0,3" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.Provenance}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           Name="Provenance"
                           FontSize="11.5"
                           Classes="provenance"
                           Classes.verified="{Binding Selected.ProvenanceIsVerified}" />
              </StackPanel>

              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.Notes}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="secondary" />

              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="24">
                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                  <TextBlock Text="LICENCE" Classes="section" Foreground="{DynamicResource InkMetaBrush}" />
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.Licence}" />
                </StackPanel>
                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                  <TextBlock Text="LANGUAGES" Classes="section" Foreground="{DynamicResource InkMetaBrush}" />
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.Languages}" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="420" />
                </StackPanel>
              </StackPanel>

              <!-- The provenance notice above tells the reader that downloading an unverified entry
                   needs an explicit opt-in. These are the controls it refers to; without them the
                   notice was pointing at nothing and the tab could not manage anything at all. -->
              <CheckBox Name="UnverifiedOptIn"
                        Content="Download this file even though Uindosill cannot check it"
                        IsChecked="{Binding AllowUnverified}"
                        IsVisible="{Binding Selected.NeedsUnverifiedOptIn}" />

              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                <Button Classes="onPage" Content="Download" Command="{Binding DownloadCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding Selected.CanDownload}" />
                <Button Classes="onPage" Content="Cancel" Command="{Binding CancelDownloadCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding Selected.IsBusy}" />
                <Button Classes="onPage" Content="Remove" Command="{Binding RemoveCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding Selected.IsInstalled}" />
              </StackPanel>

              <ProgressBar Value="{Binding Selected.Progress}" Maximum="100" Height="6"
                           IsVisible="{Binding Selected.IsBusy}" />
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.Status}" Classes="meta" />
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding StatusMessage}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="secondary" />

              <Separator />

              <!-- What is in memory, and the controls for putting it there and taking it out
                   again. Until this existed the engine was built and torn down inside one Start,
                   so there was no loaded model to name and nothing in the window said which one
                   ran. The backend row is bound here too: MainWindowViewModel has exposed Backends
                   and Backend all along and this view referenced neither, so the app was pinned to
                   its Vulkan default with no way to see or change it. -->
              <Border Classes="tinted" Padding="14">
                <StackPanel Spacing="11">
                  <TextBlock Text="LOADED MODEL" Classes="section"
                             Foreground="{DynamicResource Matcha700Brush}" />
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding LoadedSummary}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />

                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                    <TextBlock Text="Backend" Classes="secondary" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    <ComboBox ItemsSource="{Binding $parent[Window].((vm:MainWindowViewModel)DataContext).Backends}"
                              SelectedItem="{Binding $parent[Window].((vm:MainWindowViewModel)DataContext).Backend}"
                              IsEnabled="{Binding !IsLoaded}" MinWidth="132" />
                    <Button Content="Load" Command="{Binding LoadCommand}"
                            IsEnabled="{Binding CanLoad}" />
                    <Button Content="Unload" Command="{Binding UnloadCommand}"
                            IsEnabled="{Binding CanUnload}" />
                  </StackPanel>

                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding BackendNote}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="meta" />
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding $parent[Window].((vm:MainWindowViewModel)DataContext).BackendExplanation}"
                             TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="meta" />
                </StackPanel>
              </Border>

              <!-- The one amber panel in the window, and it earns the colour: an uninstall that
                   silently kept 4 GiB of weights would be a surprise either way round. -->
              <Border Background="{DynamicResource WarningPanelBrush}"
                      BorderBrush="{DynamicResource WarningPanelEdgeBrush}"
                      BorderThickness="1" Padding="12,10">
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="9">
                  <Path Stroke="{DynamicResource StatusWarningBrush}" StrokeThickness="1.5"
                        StrokeLineCap="Round" StrokeJoin="Round"
                        Data="M8 2.9l5.6 10.2H2.4z M8 6.6v3.1 M8 11.5v.1"
                        Width="15" Height="15" Stretch="Uniform" VerticalAlignment="Top" />
                  <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="warning" FontSize="11.5" MaxWidth="520"
                             Text="Uninstalling Uindosill does not delete downloaded models. They live outside the application directory on purpose — the three of them come to over 3 GiB." />
                </StackPanel>
              </Border>

            </StackPanel>
          </ScrollViewer>
        </Grid>
      </TabItem>

      <!-- ══ Licences ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           Third in the TabControl because the view models and tests index it there; third from
           the right in the switcher, which puts it last, where the design wants it. -->
      <TabItem Header="Licences">
        <DockPanel Margin="26,22">
          <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Text="Notices" Classes="title" Margin="0,0,0,13" />
          <!-- HorizontalAlignment matters here: a stretched TextBlock with a MaxWidth centres its
               own box in the leftover space, so the paragraph sat in the middle of the page under
               a left-aligned heading. -->
          <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Classes="secondary" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640"
                     HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                     LineHeight="20" Margin="0,0,0,13"
                     Text="Shown here because the licences require the notice to be present where the material is used, not only in a file in the source repository." />
          <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Text="{Binding EnvironmentSummary}" Classes="meta"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,13,0,0" />
          <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Text="{Binding ThreadingNote}" Classes="meta"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,8,0,0" />

          <Border Background="{DynamicResource SunkenBrush}"
                  BorderBrush="{DynamicResource RuleSeenBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="1" Padding="16,14">
            <ScrollViewer>
              <SelectableTextBlock Text="{Binding LicenceText}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                   Classes="mono" FontSize="11" LineHeight="19.25"
                                   Foreground="{DynamicResource InkSecondaryBrush}" />
            </ScrollViewer>
          </Border>
        </DockPanel>
      </TabItem>

      <!-- ══ Updates ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
      <TabItem Header="Updates">
        <ScrollViewer Margin="26,22" DataContext="{Binding Updates}" x:DataType="vm:UpdatesViewModel">
          <StackPanel Spacing="15" MaxWidth="700" HorizontalAlignment="Left">

            <TextBlock Text="Updates" Classes="title" />

            <TextBlock Text="{Binding CurrentVersion, StringFormat='This is version {0}.'}"
                       FontSize="13" />

            <Border Classes="tinted" Padding="14,12">
              <SelectableTextBlock Text="{Binding Status}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                   Foreground="{DynamicResource Matcha700Brush}" />
            </Border>

            <ProgressBar Minimum="0" Maximum="100" Value="{Binding Progress}"
                         IsVisible="{Binding IsBusy}" Height="6" />

            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
              <Button Classes="primary onPage" Content="Check now" Command="{Binding CheckCommand}"
                      IsEnabled="{Binding CanCheck}" />
              <Button Classes="onPage" Content="Download and restart" Name="InstallUpdate"
                      Command="{Binding InstallCommand}"
                      IsEnabled="{Binding CanInstall}" />
            </StackPanel>

            <CheckBox Content="Check for updates when Uindosill starts"
                      IsChecked="{Binding CheckOnLaunch}" />

            <Separator />

            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="secondary" FontSize="12" LineHeight="19.8"
                       Text="That check is the only thing this application does on the network without being asked. It is one HTTPS request to this project's GitHub releases page, it sends nothing about you or your files, and it downloads nothing until you press the button above. Everything else that reaches the network — downloading a model — you start yourself." />

            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="secondary" FontSize="12" LineHeight="19.8"
                       Text="Updating replaces the application's own files. Downloaded models are kept somewhere else on purpose and are not touched by an update, or by uninstalling." />

          </StackPanel>
        </ScrollViewer>
      </TabItem>

      <!-- ══ Ask ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           v2's tab, and the first surface in this application drawn in taro. Everything here is
           that colour and nothing above it is, which is the whole of the generation rule: a
           surface's colour says which product generation it belongs to.

           Two thirds of it work. The recording plays, the transcript beside it is the pipeline's
           own segments with their own times, and clicking one jumps there — none of which needs a
           language model, which is the sequencing `docs/V2-ASK-THE-TRANSCRIPT.md` asks for.

           The last third is drawn and covered. The chat panel is the shape the feature will take,
           every control in it disabled, under a notice that says it is not built. That is a
           deliberate exception to this window's own rule against shipping controls wired to
           nothing: the rule exists so that nothing here fails silently, and a panel that says in
           so many words what it cannot do is the opposite of a silent failure. What would break
           the rule is a live-looking text box that swallows a question. -->
      <TabItem Header="Ask">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="228,14,*,330" Margin="16"
              DataContext="{Binding Ask}" x:DataType="vm:AskViewModel">

          <!-- ── The queue, again ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
               The same rows the Transcribe tab is filling, not a copy of them. Everything in the
               queue, not only what has finished: a file is playable the moment it is dropped. -->
          <DockPanel Grid.Column="0">
            <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Text="RECORDINGS" Classes="section" Margin="0,0,0,9" />
            <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Classes="fine meta" Margin="0,0,0,9"
                       IsVisible="{Binding !HasRecordings}"
                       Text="Nothing in the queue yet. Drop a file on the Transcribe tab and it appears here, playable straight away." />
            <ListBox ItemsSource="{Binding Recordings}" SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedRecording}">
              <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:JobViewModel">
                  <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding FileName}" Classes="emphasis"
                               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Status}" Classes="meta" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </DataTemplate>
              </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
            </ListBox>
          </DockPanel>

          <GridSplitter Grid.Column="1" Background="Transparent" />

          <!-- ── The recording, and its words ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
          <DockPanel Grid.Column="2" Margin="0,0,14,0">

            <!-- The transport. Docked to the bottom of this column rather than floating over the
                 transcript, because it belongs to the recording rather than to the reading. -->
            <Border DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Name="Transport" Classes="tinted taro"
                    Padding="13,11" Margin="0,11,0,0">
              <StackPanel Spacing="8">
                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,13,*,Auto">

                  <!-- One of the shapes that is genuinely a circle. Two glyphs rather than one
                       that changes: a filled triangle and two bars, each visible on its own half
                       of the state, so neither is drawn by a converter nobody can test. -->
                  <Button Grid.Column="0" Name="PlayPause" Classes="transport"
                          Command="{Binding PlayPauseCommand}"
                          ToolTip.Tip="{Binding PlayPauseLabel}">
                    <Panel Width="14" Height="15">
                      <!-- Nudged right by two: a triangle centred on its bounding box reads as
                           sitting left of centre inside a circle. -->
                      <Path Data="M0,0 L13,7.5 L0,15 Z" Stretch="None" Margin="3,0,0,0"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                            IsVisible="{Binding !IsPlaying}" />
                      <Path Data="M0,0 H4 V15 H0 Z M8,0 H12 V15 H8 Z" Stretch="None"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                            IsVisible="{Binding IsPlaying}" />
                    </Panel>
                  </Button>

                  <!-- The bar, and a transparent strip over it that takes the pointer. The strip
                       is what makes the bar seekable: it knows its own width, so a press at an x
                       is a fraction of the recording, which is a question no binding can answer.
                       18px tall over a 6px bar, so the target is a comfortable one. -->
                  <Panel Grid.Column="2" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <ProgressBar Classes="seek" Minimum="0"
                                 Maximum="{Binding DurationSeconds}"
                                 Value="{Binding PositionSeconds}" />
                    <Border Name="SeekStrip" Background="Transparent" Height="18" Cursor="Hand" />
                  </Panel>

                  <StackPanel Grid.Column="3" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="5"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="13,0,0,0">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding PositionLabel}" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                               Foreground="{DynamicResource Taro700Brush}" />
                    <TextBlock Text="/" Classes="meta" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding DurationLabel}" Classes="meta" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </Grid>

                <TextBlock Text="{Binding SelectedRecording.FileName}" Classes="meta"
                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           IsVisible="{Binding SelectedRecording, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

                <!-- Why the transport is dead, when it is. A file this build cannot sound is still
                     a file whose transcript is worth reading, so the tab carries on around it. -->
                <TextBlock Name="PlaybackNotice" Text="{Binding PlaybackNotice}"
                           Classes="fine warning"
                           IsVisible="{Binding PlaybackNotice, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
              </StackPanel>
            </Border>

            <!-- ── Find a word ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
                 The reason anyone opens a three-hour transcript is usually one word. This runs on
                 v1's own data and needs no model, which is why it is here rather than waiting for
                 the panel on the right: it is useful before anything can be asked.

                 It does not seek. Finding a word and hearing it are two intentions — somebody
                 scanning for every mention of a name does not want the audio jumping under them
                 on each press of Enter — so a hit is scrolled to and marked, and clicking it is
                 what plays it. -->
            <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Top" ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" Margin="0,0,0,10"
                  IsEnabled="{Binding HasTranscript}">
              <TextBox Grid.Column="0" Name="SearchBox" Text="{Binding SearchTerm}"
                       PlaceholderText="Find a word — Enter for the next one" />
              <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="7"
                          Margin="9,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                <TextBlock Name="SearchSummary" Text="{Binding SearchSummary}" Classes="meta"
                           MinWidth="62" TextAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <Button Name="PreviousMatch" Content="Previous" ToolTip.Tip="The hit before this one"
                        Command="{Binding PreviousMatchCommand}" />
                <Button Name="NextMatch" Content="Next" ToolTip.Tip="The next hit — or press Enter"
                        Command="{Binding NextMatchCommand}" />
              </StackPanel>
            </Grid>

            <Border Classes="panel">
              <Panel>
                <ScrollViewer IsVisible="{Binding HasTranscript}" Padding="7,9">
                  <ItemsControl Name="Cues" ItemsSource="{Binding Lines}">
                    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                      <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:TranscriptLineViewModel">
                        <!-- A Button, so the keyboard reaches every cue and the seek is a command
                             like everything else in this window. Its template draws no chrome —
                             see Button.cue — so what a reader sees is a paragraph that lights up
                             under the pointer and stays lit while it is the one being played. -->
                        <Button Classes="cue" Classes.playing="{Binding IsActive}"
                                Classes.found="{Binding IsCurrentMatch}"
                                Command="{Binding $parent[ItemsControl].((vm:AskViewModel)DataContext).SeekToLineCommand}"
                                CommandParameter="{Binding}">
                          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="54,Auto,*">
                            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding Timestamp}"
                                       FontSize="11.5" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Top" Margin="0,2,0,0"
                                       Foreground="{DynamicResource Taro700Brush}" />
                            <Border Grid.Column="1" Margin="0,0,9,0"
                                    IsVisible="{Binding HasSpeaker}"
                                    Classes="speaker"
                                    Classes.chip0="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=0}"
                                    Classes.chip1="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=1}"
                                    Classes.chip2="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=2}"
                                    Classes.chip3="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=3}">
                              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Speaker}" />
                            </Border>
                            <!-- Inlines rather than Text, which is what lets one word inside a
                                 paragraph carry a ground of its own. Bound to Marked — the words
                                 and the term as one value — because a converter takes one thing,
                                 and because only the lines that carry the term raise a change for
                                 it, so a keystroke redraws the handful that matched rather than
                                 the whole transcript. -->
                            <TextBlock Grid.Column="2"
                                       Inlines="{Binding Marked, Converter={StaticResource Highlight}}"
                                       TextWrapping="Wrap" LineHeight="20.3" />
                          </Grid>
                        </Button>
                      </DataTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl>
                </ScrollViewer>

                <TextBlock Name="TranscriptNotice" Text="{Binding TranscriptNotice}"
                           IsVisible="{Binding !HasTranscript}"
                           Classes="secondary" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="380"
                           LineHeight="20" Margin="26"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           TextAlignment="Center" />
              </Panel>
            </Border>
          </DockPanel>

          <!-- ── The chat panel, drawn and not built ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
          <Border Grid.Column="3" Classes="panel">
            <Panel>
              <DockPanel Name="AskPanel" IsEnabled="False" Margin="15,14">
                <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Text="ASK THIS TRANSCRIPT" Classes="section" />

                <!-- The input, which is where a question would go. -->
                <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" ColumnDefinitions="*,8,Auto" Margin="0,12,0,0">
                  <TextBox Grid.Column="0" Name="AskInput"
                           PlaceholderText="Ask about this recording…" />
                  <Button Grid.Column="2" Name="AskSend" Content="Ask" />
                </Grid>

                <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Top" Spacing="10" Margin="0,11,0,0">
                  <TextBlock Classes="fine secondary"
                             Text="Answers would arrive as short bullets, each carrying the moment it came from — a timestamp you click to hear it, so every claim is one press from being checked." />
                  <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Suggestions}">
                    <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                      <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                        <WrapPanel ItemSpacing="6" LineSpacing="6" />
                      </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                      <DataTemplate x:DataType="sys:String">
                        <Border Classes="chip">
                          <TextBlock Text="{Binding}" />
                        </Border>
                      </DataTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl>
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- Where the conversation would be. Empty on purpose: a canned exchange here
                     would be a screenshot of a feature nobody can use. -->
                <Panel />
              </DockPanel>

              <!-- The notice, over all of it. Not a grey wash — the panel's own ground at 82%, so
                   what is underneath reads as designed-but-not-yet rather than switched off. -->
              <Border Name="AskWorkInProgress" Classes="wip">
                <StackPanel Spacing="11" MaxWidth="250" Margin="24"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                  <Border Classes="badge taro" HorizontalAlignment="Center">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding WorkInProgressTitle}" />
                  </Border>
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding WorkInProgressNotice}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                             FontSize="11.5" LineHeight="18.5" TextAlignment="Center"
                             Foreground="{DynamicResource Taro700Brush}" />
                </StackPanel>
              </Border>
            </Panel>
          </Border>
        </Grid>
      </TabItem>

    </TabControl>
  </DockPanel>
  </Border>
</Window>
